1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,466
Imagine nobody knows the root password of a server. You are locked out.

2
00:00:04,486 --> 00:00:07,458
But if you can reach the console, you are not stuck:

3
00:00:07,478 --> 00:00:09,703
you can interrupt the boot, and get in.

4
00:00:10,173 --> 00:00:14,739
In this video: interrupt the boot process in order to gain access to a system.

5
00:00:19,259 --> 00:00:21,842
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:22,312 --> 00:00:25,940
Interrupt the boot process in order to gain access to a system.

7
00:00:26,410 --> 00:00:29,629
We'll stop the boot early, reset the root password,

8
00:00:29,649 --> 00:00:31,915
and let SELinux fix the file labels.

9
00:00:41,435 --> 00:00:45,889
A Linux boot goes in stages. The firmware starts GRUB, the boot menu.

10
00:00:45,909 --> 00:00:50,557
GRUB loads the kernel and a small temporary system called the initramfs.

11
00:00:50,577 --> 00:00:52,956
Only then does the real system start.

12
00:01:07,426 --> 00:01:10,956
The option rd.break stops the boot inside the initramfs,

13
00:01:10,976 --> 00:01:13,617
just before the switch to the real system.

14
00:01:13,637 --> 00:01:16,533
There you get a root shell without a password,

15
00:01:16,553 --> 00:01:20,462
and the real root file system waits under the folder /sysroot.

16
00:01:28,932 --> 00:01:31,792
Anyone who can reach the console can do this.

17
00:01:31,812 --> 00:01:35,440
That is why console access to a server must be protected.

18
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:45,318
This is the real boot menu of our lab machine, captured from its screen.

19
00:01:45,338 --> 00:01:47,447
We press e to edit the first entry.

20
00:01:56,967 --> 00:01:59,212
The editor shows the boot commands.

21
00:01:59,232 --> 00:02:02,512
The line that starts with linux is the kernel line.

22
00:02:02,532 --> 00:02:08,595
We go down to it, press Ctrl+E to jump to the end of the line, and type a space, and rd.break.

23
00:02:22,115 --> 00:02:26,137
Here is the result: rd.break at the end of the linux line.

24
00:02:26,157 --> 00:02:30,458
This edit is for this one boot only. Now press Ctrl+X to boot.

25
00:02:41,978 --> 00:02:46,234
The kernel and the initramfs start. The initramfs prints a warning:

26
00:02:46,254 --> 00:02:49,745
break before switch root. That is our rd.break at work.

27
00:02:53,215 --> 00:02:59,148
Then it says: press Enter for maintenance. We press Enter, and we have a root shell.

28
00:02:59,168 --> 00:03:01,771
The prompt is sh-5.2 and a hash sign.

29
00:03:08,291 --> 00:03:13,330
The real root file system is mounted under /sysroot, but read-only.

30
00:03:13,350 --> 00:03:16,578
mount with -o remount,rw makes it writable.

31
00:03:16,598 --> 00:03:21,033
The -o option gives mount a list of options. remount means:

32
00:03:21,053 --> 00:03:26,319
change a file system that is already mounted. rw means read and write.

33
00:03:35,789 --> 00:03:39,032
chroot changes the root of the shell to /sysroot.

34
00:03:39,052 --> 00:03:41,895
From now on, a slash means the real system,

35
00:03:41,915 --> 00:03:45,092
and commands like passwd work on the real files.

36
00:03:52,612 --> 00:03:55,125
passwd root sets a new password for root.

37
00:03:55,145 --> 00:03:58,091
It asks twice, and shows nothing while you type.

38
00:03:58,111 --> 00:04:00,933
Password updated successfully means it worked.

39
00:04:06,403 --> 00:04:10,471
Now touch /.autorelabel. touch creates an empty file.

40
00:04:10,491 --> 00:04:13,864
We changed a file that has no SELinux label,

41
00:04:13,884 --> 00:04:18,260
and this flag file tells the next boot to fix all labels.

42
00:04:18,280 --> 00:04:20,805
ls -l shows that the file exists.

43
00:04:29,275 --> 00:04:33,088
Type exit to leave the chroot, and exit again to leave the shell.

44
00:04:33,108 --> 00:04:37,276
The boot continues, and the relabel runs, so this first boot is slower.

45
00:04:42,796 --> 00:04:46,232
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

46
00:04:47,752 --> 00:04:53,279
The machine relabels, and starts normally. We log in over ssh, and check.

47
00:04:53,299 --> 00:04:56,927
passwd -S root shows P: root has a password now.

48
00:05:00,397 --> 00:05:03,984
getenforce says Enforcing, so SELinux is still on.

49
00:05:04,004 --> 00:05:09,466
ls says there is no /.autorelabel any more, because the relabel is finished.

50
00:05:09,486 --> 00:05:11,342
And the system is running.

51
00:05:18,862 --> 00:05:20,455
Two things to remember.

52
00:05:20,475 --> 00:05:25,084
If you forget touch /.autorelabel, SELinux can block logins later,

53
00:05:25,104 --> 00:05:28,591
because the new password file has the wrong label.

54
00:05:34,061 --> 00:05:36,921
And the boot menu edit lives for one boot only.

55
00:05:36,941 --> 00:05:41,700
Your own machine may show a slightly different menu, but the idea is the same.

56
00:05:47,220 --> 00:05:53,213
Your turn. On a practice machine where you have the console, edit the boot entry, add rd.break,

57
00:05:53,233 --> 00:05:58,024
and boot. Reset the root password, set the autorelabel flag, and exit twice.

58
00:05:58,044 --> 00:06:00,683
When the machine is back, run the checker.

59
00:06:07,153 --> 00:06:08,903
Try before you open the solution.

60
00:06:14,423 --> 00:06:19,992
Next up: identify CPU and memory intensive processes, and kill processes.

61
00:06:20,462 --> 00:06:23,365
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
